Inversion of Control (IoC) in JavaScript means:

You give control of when and how your function is executed to another function or system.

This is one of the important problems associated with callbacks.

Simple example
function fetchData(callback) {
    // Some asynchronous operation
    setTimeout(() => {
        callback("Data received");
    }, 2000);
}

fetchData((data) => {
    console.log(data);
});

Here, you don't decide when the callback runs.

You give your callback to fetchData():

(data) => {
    console.log(data);
}

And fetchData() decides:

when to call it
whether to call it
potentially how many times to call it
what data to pass to it

That's Inversion of Control.

Why is it called "inversion"?

Normally, you might think:

My code → controls → when function executes

With a callback:

My code → gives function → to another function
                         ↓
                  controls execution

The control has effectively been handed over.

Real-world analogy

Imagine you give your phone to a delivery company and say:

"Call me when my package arrives."

You no longer control when they call you.

The delivery company controls that.

Similarly:

setTimeout(() => {
    console.log("Done");
}, 2000);

You provide the callback, but setTimeout controls when it executes.
